<section class="v3-pagehero">
  <div class="v3-wrap">
    <p class="v3-eyebrow">Product</p>
    <h1>Nexus — POS and loyalty, built for operators with more than one counter</h1>
    <p class="v3-pagehero__lead">Most POS systems in Singapore price and report per outlet. Open a second shop and you get a second island: two sets of reports, two customer lists, and a regular who visits both looks like two strangers. Nexus is built the other way round.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="v3-section">
  <div class="v3-wrap">
    <div class="v3-section__head">
      <p class="v3-kicker">The idea</p>
      <h2>POS is per outlet. Loyalty is per brand.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="v3-cards v3-cards--3">
      <div class="v3-card">
        <div class="v3-card__num">01</div>
        <h3>One customer, however many doors</h3>
        <p>Customers are held at company level, not at the till. Someone who buys at your Selegie shop and your Robinson Road stand is one person with one balance — not two accounts that never meet.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="v3-card">
        <div class="v3-card__num">02</div>
        <h3>You choose the shape</h3>
        <p>One points balance pooled across all your brands, a separate balance per brand, or a mix of both. That is a setting, not a custom build and not a quotation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="v3-card">
        <div class="v3-card__num">03</div>
        <h3>We build it and we run it</h3>
        <p>The same team that wrote it operates it in production. No hand-off to a support queue that has never seen the code.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="v3-section v3-section--surface">
  <div class="v3-wrap">
    <div class="v3-section__head">
      <p class="v3-kicker">What it does</p>
      <h2>On the counter, and behind it</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="v3-cards v3-cards--3">
      <div class="v3-card">
        <div class="v3-card__num">01</div>
        <h3>Point of sale</h3>
        <p>Full till on a tablet. Tap-to-pay, multiple printers, QR ordering from the table, and reports per outlet so a junior manager can read their own shop without reading everyone else's.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="v3-card">
        <div class="v3-card__num">02</div>
        <h3>Loyalty</h3>
        <p>Customer wallet, stamp cards, points, birthday bonus and referrals. Priced for the brand, so opening a third outlet does not open a third loyalty programme.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="v3-card">
        <div class="v3-card__num">03</div>
        <h3>Payments, itemised</h3>
        <p>The payment provider's rate and our platform fee are always shown as separate lines, never blended into the software price. You can audit every cent of it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="v3-section">
  <div class="v3-wrap">
    <div class="v3-section__head">
      <p class="v3-kicker">Straight answers</p>
      <h2>What Nexus does not do</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="v3-cards v3-cards--3">
      <div class="v3-card">
        <div class="v3-card__num">01</div>
        <h3>Wholesale and B2B invoicing</h3>
        <p>If your wholesale accounts and invoices live somewhere else, they stay there. We are the retail counter and the customer side, not your B2B ledger.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="v3-card">
        <div class="v3-card__num">02</div>
        <h3>Time-scheduled menus</h3>
        <p>If your menu changes from cafe to bar at a set hour, today that is a manual switch on the tablet, not an automatic one.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="v3-card">
        <div class="v3-card__num">03</div>
        <h3>Member limits, honestly</h3>
        <p>Entry-level loyalty covers up to 5,000 active members, checked by hand at renewal rather than metered live in the product. We would rather write that here than have you discover it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

